exercises.solution:
```python
def is_group(elements, op):
    s = set(elements)
    if any(op(a, b) not in s for a in elements for b in elements):
        return False
    if any(op(op(a, b), c) != op(a, op(b, c))
           for a in elements for b in elements for c in elements):
        return False
    ids = [e for e in elements
           if all(op(e, a) == a == op(a, e) for a in elements)]
    if not ids:
        return False
    e = ids[0]
    return all(any(op(a, b) == e == op(b, a) for b in elements)
               for a in elements)
```

By Theorem 1 there is at most one identity, so taking `ids[0]` loses nothing. Checking associativity takes $n^3$ evaluations — feasible for small tables, but it is exactly the kind of check that a proof does once for all sizes.
